</nav></div></header><section class="article-hero"><div class="wrap"><div class="crumb"><a href="../thought-leadership">Thought Leadership</a> / Time &amp; Labor Data</div><div class="cat">Time &amp; Labor Data</div><h1>Job Codes, Cost Centers &amp; Worktags at the Clock: Capture Labor Detail Without Slowing Employees Down</h1><p>Workday can represent a sophisticated labor and costing model. The employee should not have to navigate that complexity at the clock. The design challenge is to present the right value, to the right worker, at the right moment.</p><div class="meta"><span>ZKTeco WFM Insights</span><span>Educational Perspective</span><span>Updated September 2026</span></div></div></section><main class="content-wrap"><article class="article-body"><p class="lead">Enterprise labor structures are built for reporting, costing and business control. Employee punch screens are built for speed and accuracy. A strong workforce-data design connects the two without exposing the full complexity of the enterprise model to the worker.</p>
<h2>The clock should not become a miniature ERP screen</h2><p>Workday can use worktags to categorize time for cost centers, projects, locations, programs, grants and other reporting dimensions. Those structures can be powerful downstream. At the workforce edge, however, showing every possible value can create the exact data-quality problem the organization is trying to solve.</p>
<div class="tl-scenario"><div class="eyebrow">Operating example</div><h2>500 cost centers do not belong on a 7-inch screen</h2><p>A company has hundreds of valid cost centers across the enterprise. One warehouse employee can legitimately work in only four. Presenting all 500 values increases search time and selection risk. Presenting four relevant choices makes the same labor architecture usable.</p><p>The design goal is not fewer enterprise values. It is fewer irrelevant employee choices.</p></div>
<h2>Start with ownership</h2><p>For each labor dimension, determine which system is authoritative. Workday may own cost centers, projects, jobs or worktags. The collection layer should generally consume approved values rather than creating a competing master-data model.</p><p>Then define how that value becomes relevant to a specific employee, site or workflow.</p>
<h2>Five ways to simplify labor capture</h2><div class="tl-grid"><div class="tl-card"><h3>1. Default</h3><p>Automatically apply the normal job or department and ask only when the employee needs to change it.</p></div><div class="tl-card"><h3>2. Filter</h3><p>Show only values relevant to the employee, device, location or role.</p></div><div class="tl-card"><h3>3. Hierarchy</h3><p>Use an understandable sequence such as Department → Job instead of one enormous flat list.</p></div><div class="tl-card"><h3>4. Recent / favorite</h3><p>Surface common selections quickly where the business process allows it.</p></div><div class="tl-card"><h3>5. Scan or shortcut</h3><p>For repetitive industrial workflows, a barcode, QR or configured shortcut may reduce selection steps.</p></div></div>
<h2>Worktag precedence makes source selection important</h2><p>Workday documentation notes that worktag values entered or imported with time can take precedence over defaulting rules. That means the value selected at the workforce edge can materially affect downstream costing.</p><p>This makes interface design a control issue—not merely a convenience issue.</p>
<div class="tl-overlook"><div class="eyebrow">What most buyers overlook</div><h2>Effective dating and change management matter</h2><p>A labor value that was correct yesterday may be invalid tomorrow. Projects close. Cost centers change. Employees transfer. A production design needs a disciplined synchronization and filtering model so workers do not see stale values or lose access to newly valid ones.</p></div>
<h2>Offline changes the question</h2><p>If the clock loses connectivity, what labor choices remain available? A good offline design balances continuity with data integrity. It should not assume that every cloud-side change can be known while disconnected.</p><p>Organizations should explicitly define which labor selections must remain available offline and how those events are reconciled when connectivity returns.</p>
<div class="tl-scenario"><div class="eyebrow">Operating example</div><h2>A project opens this morning. Does the employee see it?</h2><p>A new customer project becomes active in Workday before the first shift. A technician is expected to charge time to it that afternoon. The clock is online, the employee is active, and the project exists in Workday—but the employee still cannot select it.</p><p>The issue may be synchronization timing, filtering, effective dating, an employee assignment, or a configuration dependency. This illustrates why labor-code design is not only about the screen. It is a <strong>master-data lifecycle</strong> from Workday to the workforce edge and back again.</p></div>
<h2>Design labor choices as a controlled data pipeline</h2>
<div class="tl-flow"><div><span>1</span>Workday master data</div><div><span>2</span>Eligibility / filtering</div><div><span>3</span>Employee choice</div><div><span>4</span>Time + labor event</div><div><span>5</span>Workday result</div></div>
<p>Each stage should have an owner. Workday may own the approved value. The collection layer determines which values are relevant to the employee. The employee interaction captures the choice. The integration then returns the event with the labor context needed downstream.</p>
<h2>Use case: simplify a complex project environment</h2>
<p>Imagine a service organization with 250 active projects. One technician is assigned to six. Presenting all 250 values makes search slower and selection risk higher. Presenting the six valid projects—perhaps with the technician's most recent choice near the top—preserves the enterprise structure while making the interaction practical.</p>
<p>For another employee who always works one cost center, the best interface may show no cost-center question at all. The correct value can be defaulted.</p>
<div class="tl-overlook"><div class="eyebrow">What most buyers overlook</div><h2>The hard part is not storing the code. It is governing the code through change.</h2><p>Projects close. Cost centers are reorganized. Employees transfer. Temporary assignments begin and end. If the workforce edge receives stale or overly broad master data, the screen can remain technically functional while producing incorrect choices.</p><p>Ask how new values become available, how inactive values disappear, what happens to transactions already in progress, and how support can reconstruct which choices were valid at the time of the punch.</p></div>
<h2>Test the lifecycle—not just today's list</h2>
<ul><li>Create a new labor value and confirm when it appears for the right population.</li><li>Remove an employee's eligibility and confirm the value disappears appropriately.</li><li>Inactivate a project and test what happens to a stored or offline transaction.</li><li>Transfer an employee to another location or role and confirm the available choices change.</li><li>Verify the selected labor context arrives in Workday as expected.</li></ul>
<div class="tl-rule"><div class="eyebrow">Decision rule</div><strong>Keep the enterprise data model rich and the employee interaction narrow.</strong><p>The clock should expose only the valid choices needed to create the correct workforce event—not the full Workday hierarchy.</p></div>
<h2>Use employee language—even when the backend uses technical labels</h2><p>The data model may call a value a worktag, cost center, project or organization. The employee may know it as “Packaging,” “Customer 417,” “Line 3” or “North Campus.” The workforce interaction should use labels employees recognize while still transmitting the correct enterprise value behind the scenes.</p><p>This sounds cosmetic, but it is a data-quality control. If two backend values have names employees cannot distinguish, wrong selections become predictable. Clear labels, logical grouping and role-based filtering reduce the need for training and downstream correction.</p><p>When designing the screen, ask a frontline employee to explain every choice without using implementation terminology. If the choice only makes sense to the project team, the interface is not finished.</p><h2>Questions leaders should ask</h2><div class="questions"><ul><li>Which labor dimensions are actually required at the point of collection?</li><li>Which system owns each value?</li><li>How are allowed values filtered for each employee or site?</li><li>Can normal values be defaulted?</li><li>How many taps does the typical transfer require?</li><li>How quickly do new or changed worktags reach the clock?</li><li>What happens when a value becomes inactive?</li><li>Which labor selections remain available offline?</li><li>Can support explain which value was presented and selected at the time of the transaction?</li></ul></div>
<div class="tl-rule"><div class="eyebrow">Decision rule</div><strong>Make enterprise complexity invisible to the employee.</strong><p>Preserve the richness of Workday's labor model while limiting the workforce interaction to the smallest set of valid choices.</p></div>
<div class="perspective"><div class="label">ZKTeco WFM perspective</div><h2>Keep the enterprise structure rich and the employee interaction simple.</h2><p>ZKTeco WFM does not treat labor-code support as a long drop-down list on a clock. The better design is to use the employee, location and approved workflow to present the smallest useful set of choices at the workforce edge. An enterprise may maintain hundreds of jobs, projects, cost centers or Workday worktags, but a specific employee may need only a handful during a particular shift.</p><p>Ultima and TimeTrack provide the interaction layer where that simplification can happen. Depending on the customer configuration, employees can select approved labor values, perform transfers and create workforce events that carry the context required downstream. In a Workday environment, CirrusDCS supports the synchronization and event flow that connects those interactions with Workday. ZKTeco WFM implementation teams can also evaluate customer-specific hierarchy and workflow requirements rather than assuming every organization should use the same labor-selection model.</p><p>The value is not merely that the clock can display a code. It is that <strong>complex enterprise labor structures can be translated into a practical point-of-work experience</strong>—helping the employee make the right selection without turning the endpoint into an accounting application.</p></div><div class="takeaway"><h2>Key takeaway</h2><p>The labor hierarchy can be complex; the employee interaction should not be. Start with the data the business actually needs, determine which choices are relevant to each workforce population, and expose only those choices at the right moment. A well-designed clock workflow preserves rich labor context for Workday or another WFM/HCM platform while keeping the transaction fast enough for real employees in real operating environments.</p></div>
